---
import '../styles/global.css'
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css'
import katex from 'katex'
import { settingsBootScript } from '@lib/settings'
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro'
import SeoHead from '../components/SeoHead.astro'
import { breadcrumbLd } from '@lib/seo'

// inline and display math, rendered at build time
const m = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: false })
const M = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: false, displayMode: true })
// a sentence with inline $…$ math, rendered as one string so spaces around the math are kept
const t = (text: string) => text.replace(/\$([^$]+)\$/g, (_, x) => m(x))
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <SeoHead title="Math you need · LLM by Hand" description="Every piece of math the course uses, on one page, each with a small worked example."
      jsonLd={breadcrumbLd([['LLM by Hand', '/'], ['Math you need', '/math/']])} page={{ kind: 'math' }} />
    <script is:inline set:html={settingsBootScript} />
    <style>
      .wrap { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem; }
      .top { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; }
      .top .tools { display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; }
      .crumb { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--dim); text-decoration: none; }
      .crumb:hover { color: var(--accent); }
      h1 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.2rem); margin: 0.3rem 0 0.2rem; }
      .lede { color: var(--dim); margin: 0 0 1.2rem; max-width: 62ch; }
      .toc { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 1rem; font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
      h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 2.4rem 0 0.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 1rem; }
      p, li { max-width: 66ch; }
      .ex { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
        background: var(--card); margin: 0.8rem 0; }
      .ex b.k { font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--accent); letter-spacing: 0.04em; display: block; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
      .ex .katex-display { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
      .toc a { color: var(--fg); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
      .toc a:hover { color: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }
      .scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
      table { width: auto; }
      td, th { text-align: center; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
      .where { color: var(--dim); font-size: 0.88rem; }
      .where a { color: inherit; }
      details.try { border: 1px dashed var(--line); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem; margin: 0.8rem 0; }
      details.try summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent); }
      details.try p { margin: 0.4rem 0 0.2rem; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />
    <div class="wrap">

      <h1>Math you need, on one page</h1>
      <p class="lede">This is all the math the course uses. Each idea has one small example with real numbers.
        Read it once now, or come back when a level sends you here. Each section ends with a short “Try it”: compute the answer on paper first, then open it to check.</p>
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#exp-ln">e, exp and ln</a><a href="#powers">Powers and roots</a><a href="#sum">Σ, the sum sign</a>
        <a href="#mean-std">Mean, variance, std</a><a href="#derivative">Derivative</a><a href="#chain-rule">Chain rule</a>
        <a href="#sin-poly">sin, cos and polynomials</a><a href="#scientific">1e−5 and powers of ten</a><a href="#shapes">Vectors and shapes</a>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="exp-ln">e, exp and ln</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`$e \\approx 2.72$ is a fixed number, like $\\pi$. $e^x$ (in code <code>np.exp(x)</code>) is “e multiplied by itself x times”. It works for any x, not only whole numbers, and it is always positive.`)} />
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>−2</th><th>−1</th><th>0</th><th>0.5</th><th>1</th><th>2</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><th><Fragment set:html={m('e^x')} /></th><td>0.14</td><td>0.37</td><td>1</td><td>1.65</td><td>2.72</td><td>7.39</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p set:html={t(`“Multiplied by itself x times” only makes sense for whole numbers. For x = 0, x = 0.5 or a negative x, just read the table: $e^0 = 1$ and $e^{0.5} \\approx 1.65$.`)} />
      <p set:html={t(`A negative power means “one over”: $e^{-1} = 1/e \\approx 0.37$. And $e^0 = 1$.`)} />
      <p set:html={t(`<b>ln</b> (the natural logarithm, <code>np.log</code> in code) undoes $e^x$: it answers “e to what power gives this number?”. So $\\ln 1 = 0$, $\\ln e = 1$, $\\ln 2 \\approx 0.69$ and $\\ln 10 \\approx 2.30$. ln only takes positive numbers, and ln of a number below 1 is negative.`)} />
      <p>Four rules cover everything in the course:</p>
      <Fragment set:html={M('e^{a+b} = e^a \\cdot e^b \\qquad \\ln(ab) = \\ln a + \\ln b \\qquad \\ln(1/x) = -\\ln x \\qquad \\ln(e^x) = x')} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$-\\ln 0.1 = -\\ln(1/10) = \\ln 10 \\approx 2.30$. And $\\ln(2 \\times 5) = \\ln 2 + \\ln 5$: ln turns a product into a sum. That is why a loss over many words can add up one $-\\ln p$ per word.`)} /></div>
      <p set:html={t(`Level 2 draws its 3D loss surface with height $\\ln(1 + \\text{loss})$. Adding 1 keeps the number positive, and ln pulls big values down a lot: $\\ln(1 + 0) = 0$, $\\ln(1 + 9) \\approx 2.30$, $\\ln(1 + 99) \\approx 4.61$. A bigger loss is still higher.`)} />
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what is <Fragment set:html={m('-\\ln 0.25')} />? (Use <Fragment set:html={m('\\ln 4 \\approx 1.39')} />.)</summary>
        <p set:html={t(`$0.25 = 1/4$, so $-\\ln 0.25 = -\\ln(1/4) = \\ln 4 \\approx 1.39$.`)} /></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/neurons-and-loss/">level 3</a> (cross-entropy), <a href="/learn/probability/">level 10</a> (softmax, perplexity), <a href="/learn/language-models/">level 11</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="powers">Powers and square roots</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`$x^2 = x \\times x$ and $x^3 = x \\times x \\times x$. A square is never negative: $(-3)^2 = (-3)\\times(-3) = 9$. The square root $\\sqrt{x}$ (<code>np.sqrt</code>) undoes a square: $\\sqrt{9} = 3$. It always means the positive root.`)} />
      <p set:html={t(`In Python a power is written <code>**</code>: <code>x ** 2</code> is $x^2$, and on a NumPy array <code>a ** 2</code> squares every element. Don't write <code>x ^ 2</code>: in Python <code>^</code> means something else, and on floats it gives an error.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$0.25^5 = (1/4)^5 = 1/1024$: multiply 0.25 by itself five times. A small number raised to a power shrinks fast.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what is <code>(-2) ** 2 + 3 ** 2</code>, and what is its square root?</summary>
        <p>(−2) × (−2) = 4 and 3 × 3 = 9, so the sum is 13 and its square root is √13 ≈ 3.61.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/gradient-descent/">level 2</a> (squared error), <a href="/learn/backprop/">level 6</a> (the slope of tanh), <a href="/learn/optimization/">level 7</a> (Adam).</p>

      <h2 id="sum">Σ, the sum sign</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`$\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} x_i$ means “add $x_i$ for every i from 0 to n − 1”. The small number under a letter is its position, counted from 0 as in code: $x_0$ is the first x. (Many math books count from 1 instead; the sum is the same.)`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`With x = [2, 5, 1]: $\\sum_i x_i = 2 + 5 + 1 = 8$. In NumPy that is <code>x.sum()</code>.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: with x = [4, −1, 3], what is <Fragment set:html={m('\\sum_i x_i^2')} />?</summary>
        <p>16 + 1 + 9 = 26. Square first, then add.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/matrices/">level 1</a> (each cell of a matrix product is a sum), <a href="/learn/probability/">level 10</a> (softmax divides by a sum).</p>

      <h2 id="mean-std">Mean, variance and standard deviation</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`The <b>mean</b> is the sum divided by how many numbers there are. The <b>variance</b> is the mean of the squared distances from the mean. The <b>standard deviation</b> (std) is the square root of the variance: a typical distance from the mean, in the same units as the numbers.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`x = [1, 3, 5, 7]. Mean = 16 / 4 = 4. Distances from 4: −3, −1, 1, 3. Squares: 9, 1, 1, 9. Variance = 20 / 4 = 5. std = $\\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.24$.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what are the mean and the std of <span class="vec-nw">[2, 4, 6, 8]</span>?</summary>
        <p>Mean = 20 / 4 = 5. Distances: −3, −1, 1, 3. Squares: 9, 1, 1, 9. Variance = 20 / 4 = 5, so std = √5 ≈ 2.24. (The same spacing as the example, so the same std.)</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/optimization/">level 7</a> (starting weights), <a href="/learn/probability/">level 10</a> (Gaussian noise), <a href="/learn/transformer-parts/">level 16</a> and <a href="/learn/residuals-and-norms/">N2</a> (LayerNorm), <a href="/learn/noise-and-denoise/">D1</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="derivative">The derivative: a slope</h2>
      <p>The <b>derivative</b> of a function says how fast its output changes when its input changes a tiny bit. It is the
        slope of its graph at that point. You can measure it with two numbers: change the input a little, divide the change in
        output by the change in input.</p>
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$f(x) = x^2$ at x = 3. Try x = 3.01: $f(3.01) = 9.0601$. The output grew by 0.0601 when the input grew by 0.01, so the slope is about $0.0601 / 0.01 \\approx 6$. The rule “slope of $x^2$ is $2x$” gives exactly $2 \\times 3 = 6$.`)} /></div>
      <p set:html={t(`When a function has several inputs, the <b>partial derivative</b> $\\partial L / \\partial w$ is the slope when only w moves and the other inputs stay fixed. All the partial derivatives together are the <b>gradient</b>.`)} />
      <p set:html={t(`For a function with one input, the derivative is often written $f'(x)$, read “f prime of x”. For $f(x) = x^2$, $f'(x) = 2x$, so $f'(3) = 6$. A layer’s $f'$ in level 4 is the slope of its activation function.`)} />
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: <Fragment set:html={m('f(x) = x^2')} /> at x = 5. What is f(5.01) − f(5), and so what is the slope?</summary>
        <p>f(5.01) = 25.1001, so the change is 0.1001. Divided by 0.01 that is about 10, and the rule gives 2 × 5 = 10.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/gradient-descent/">level 2</a> onward. Training is “move each parameter against its slope”.</p>

      <h2 id="chain-rule">The chain rule</h2>
      <p>If y depends on u, and u depends on x, then the slope of y with respect to x is the two slopes multiplied:</p>
      <Fragment set:html={M('\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx}')} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b>
        u = 3x and y = u². At x = 1: u = 3. The slope of y with respect to u is 2u = 6. The slope of u with respect to x is 3.
        So the slope of y with respect to x is 6 × 3 = 18. Check: y = (3x)² = 9x², whose slope 18x is 18 at x = 1.
      </div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: u = 2x and y = u². What is the slope of y with respect to x at x = 3?</summary>
        <p>u = 6. dy/du = 2u = 12, du/dx = 2, so dy/dx = 12 × 2 = 24. Check: y = 4x², slope 8x = 24 at x = 3.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/gradient-descent/">level 2</a> (where the gradient formula comes from), <a href="/learn/backprop/">level 6</a> (backpropagation is this rule, over and over).</p>

      <h2 id="sin-poly">sin, cos and polynomials</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`<b>sin</b> (<code>np.sin</code>) is a wave: it goes up and down between −1 and 1 forever. $\\sin 0 = 0$, and it repeats every $2\\pi \\approx 6.28$. Level 8 uses $y = \\sin(\\pi x)$ as a smooth curve to learn: for x from −1 to 1 it goes down to −1, back through 0, up to 1 and back to 0.`)} />
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>x</th><th>−1</th><th>−0.5</th><th>0</th><th>0.5</th><th>1</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><th><Fragment set:html={m('\\sin(\\pi x)')} /></th><td>0</td><td>−1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p set:html={t(`<b>cos</b> (<code>np.cos</code>) is the same wave, shifted by a quarter turn: $\\cos 0 = 1$. Angles in sin and cos are in <b>radians</b>, not degrees. A full turn is $2\\pi \\approx 6.28$ radians, and a quarter turn is $\\pi/2 \\approx 1.57$. So 1 radian is about 57°.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$\\cos 1 \\approx 0.54$ and $\\sin 1 \\approx 0.84$. At a quarter turn, $\\cos(\\pi/2) = 0$ and $\\sin(\\pi/2) = 1$.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what are <Fragment set:html={m('\\cos \\pi')} /> and <Fragment set:html={m('\\sin \\pi')} />? (π is half a turn.)</summary>
        <p>Half a turn points the other way: cos π = −1 and sin π = 0.</p></details>
      <p set:html={t(`A <b>polynomial</b> is a sum of powers of x, each times a number called a <b>coefficient</b>: $c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2 + \\dots$ Its <b>degree</b> is the biggest power. A degree-1 polynomial is a straight line; a higher degree can bend more times.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$1 + 2x + 3x^2$ has degree 2 and coefficients 1, 2, 3. At x = 2 it is $1 + 4 + 12 = 17$.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what is <Fragment set:html={m('2 - x + x^3')} /> at x = 2, and what is its degree?</summary>
        <p>2 − 2 + 8 = 8. The biggest power is 3, so the degree is 3.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/generalization/">level 8</a> (fitting a curve with too many parameters), <a href="/learn/transformer-parts/">level 16</a> (position encodings use sin and cos), <a href="/learn/modern-llm/">level 19</a> (RoPE turns vectors by angles in radians).</p>

      <h2 id="scientific">Very small and very large numbers: 1e−5 and powers of ten</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`Computers write <code>1e-5</code> for $1 \\times 10^{-5} = 0.00001$ and <code>3e4</code> for $3 \\times 10^{4} = 30000$. The number after the “e” says how many places to move the decimal point: a minus sign moves it to the left (a small number), a plus or no sign moves it to the right (a big number). This “e” has nothing to do with $e \\approx 2.72$.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b>
        <code>2e-11</code> = 0.00000000002. <code>1.5e3</code> = 1500.
      </div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: write <code>1e-3</code> and <code>4e2</code> as plain numbers.</summary>
        <p>1e-3 = 0.001 (three places to the left). 4e2 = 400 (two places to the right).</p></details>
      <p set:html={t(`To multiply powers of ten, add the exponents: $10^3 \\times 10^4 = 10^7$. To divide them, subtract the exponents: $10^{14} / 10^{10} = 10^4$. With a number in front, divide that number separately.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b><span set:html={t(`$10^{14} / (1.4 \\times 10^{10}) = 10^4 / 1.4 \\approx 7{,}143$. And $1.4 \\times 10^{10} / 10^{12} = 1.4 \\times 10^{-2} = 0.014$.`)} /></div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what is <Fragment set:html={m('2.8 \\times 10^{13} / 10^{14}')} />?</summary>
        <p>Subtract the exponents: 13 − 14 = −1. So it is 2.8 × 10⁻¹ = 0.28.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/backprop/">level 6</a> (checking a gradient with a tiny step), <a href="/learn/numbers-in-a-computer/">U2</a>, <a href="/learn/inference-cost/">level 20</a> (memory and speed of a large model).</p>

      <h2 id="shapes">Vectors, matrices and shapes</h2>
      <p set:html={t(`A <b>vector</b> is a list of numbers, like [2, 0]. A <b>matrix</b> is a table of numbers; its shape (rows, columns) says how big it is. Rows and columns count from 0. Matrix multiplication <code>A @ B</code> needs the inner sizes to match: $(n, k)\\,@\\,(k, m) \\to (n, m)$. In formulas, two matrices written next to each other with nothing between them, like $X W$, mean the same product <code>X @ W</code>. Level 1 teaches all of this from scratch.`)} />
      <div class="ex"><b class="k">EXAMPLE</b>
        [1, 2] @ [[3], [4]] = 1×3 + 2×4 = 11. Shapes: (1, 2) @ (2, 1) → (1, 1).
      </div>
      <details class="try"><summary>Try it: what shape does (4, 3) @ (3, 2) give? And (4, 3) @ (2, 3)?</summary>
        <p>(4, 2): the inner sizes are both 3. The second is an error: the inner sizes 3 and 2 don't match.</p></details>
      <p class="where">Used in: <a href="/learn/matrices/">level 1</a> and every level after it.</p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
